course: religion-ethics
lesson: 99
title: Olympiad dilemmas: friends, truth and telling
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In an ethics olympiad you meet dilemmas where truth, loyalty, kindness and safety pull different ways, and the judges reward the answer that gives reasons and weighs both sides.
explain: Sam is hiding from bullies in the toilets, and the bullies ask Leo, "Where's Sam?"; Leo doesn't owe bullies information, so "Not telling you" and fetching a teacher is often best, and most people think even a lie here would not be wrong.
explain: The Bible has hard cases too: Rahab hid the Israelite spies and misled the king's men (Joshua 2:4-6), and the Hebrew midwives misled Pharaoh to save baby boys (Exodus 1:15-21); Christians still disagree whether the lies themselves were right.
explain: Some answers are not open: if a friend is frightened of being hurt at home, a trusted adult needs to know, and if an innocent person is blamed, loyalty to the guilty friend has limits.
explain: For the open ones, "it depends, and here are my reasons" is often the best answer, as long as you name what matters on each side.
fact: Rahab hid the spies and misled the king's men (Joshua 2:4-6), and Hebrews 11:31 praises her faith; the midwives misled Pharaoh, and God was kind to them (Exodus 1:15-21).
fact: Kant said you must not lie even to a murderer at the door; most philosophers disagree and say protecting a person comes first; many suggest refusing to answer and getting help.
fact: Jesus said to speak to the person privately first, then bring others (Matthew 18:15-16); "Anyone who has been stealing must steal no longer" (Ephesians 4:28).
fact: When someone might be in danger, safety comes first: say you can't help, don't point, and get an adult or call for help.
fact: "Defend the weak and the fatherless; uphold the cause of the poor and the oppressed" (Psalm 82:3); if a child is frightened of being hurt at home, a trusted adult needs to know.
fact: "Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves" (Proverbs 31:8); if a grown-up cheats, tell your parents so they can raise it.
fact: Carers of people with dementia (an illness that makes people forget) disagree: many say it is kinder to go along gently than to make them grieve again each time; others worry it disrespects them.
fact: "Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong" (Exodus 23:2); in famous experiments in the 1950s, many people agreed with a group's obviously wrong answer just to fit in.
fact: "Do not pervert justice or show partiality" (Deuteronomy 16:19); judges should step back when a friend takes part, which is called a conflict of interest.
fact: Some Christians say a dodge is better than even a small surprise fib; most people think fibs that protect a surprise are harmless; Kant still says no lie at all.
fact: "Acquitting the guilty and condemning the innocent: the Lord detests them both" (Proverbs 17:15).
fact: Augustine and Aquinas taught that every lie is wrong; many others point to Rahab and the midwives; all agree truthfulness is God's will.
fact: Most agree a lie needs a very strong reason, like saving a life; olympiad judges reward the team that weighs both sides.
fact: Slang check: "narc" means a telltale; a "fiver" is a £5 note.
exercise: Bullies ask Leo, "Where's Sam?" Sam is hiding in the toilets. What is the best-reasoned answer? | Refuse to tell them and fetch a teacher; he doesn't owe bullies information | Tell them where Sam is, because lying is always wrong | Join the bullies so they don't pick on him | Tell them Sam went home, then do nothing
exercise: What did Rahab do with the Israelite spies? | Hid them and misled the king's men | Handed them straight over to the king | Told them to go straight back home across the river | Sent them down to Pharaoh in Egypt as prisoners
exercise: What does Hebrews 11:31 praise about Rahab? | Her faith | Her lie | Her wealth | Her house
exercise: Leo sees his best friend Zac take a fiver from the teacher's desk. What is the best-reasoned first step? | Give Zac a chance to put it back and own up, and offer to go with him | Say nothing ever, because Zac is his best friend and friends stick together | Tell the whole class straight away | Take the fiver himself to stop Zac
exercise: A frightened girl runs past Tom. A man shouts, "Which way did she go?" What is safest? | Say he can't help, don't point, and get an adult or call for help | Point the way, because adults must always be obeyed, whatever they ask | Run after the girl himself to warn her | Ask the man why, then decide
exercise: Mia's friend is scared of her dad's temper and begs Mia to say she was at her house. What should Mia do? | Not lie, but tell a trusted adult about the fear | Lie for her, because her friend asked her to and was crying | Tell her friend to stop being scared, as dads are always right | Tell the dad exactly what her friend said about him
exercise: Kai's coach lied about a player's age to win. What should Kai do? | Tell his parents so they can raise it with the club | Say nothing, because the coach is an adult and knows the rules | Lie about his own age too | Shout at the coach in front of everyone
exercise: Great-Gran has dementia and keeps asking for her dog, who died years ago. What is the wise view? | It's an open question; let grown-ups decide, and share happy memories | Tell her every time, because the truth always comes first, whatever it costs | Laugh and tell her she's being silly | Buy a new dog and say it's the old one
exercise: The class plans to tell the head a lie about extra break. "Don't be a narc," says Brody. What should you do? | You needn't report anyone, but don't lie yourself if asked | Join the lie so you're not left out | Tell the head before anyone else speaks, so you get the credit | Stay home from school tomorrow
exercise: What did famous 1950s experiments show? | Many people agreed with an obviously wrong group answer just to fit in | People never go along with a group | Groups are always right | Everyone always disagrees with groups, just to show they're clever and different
exercise: Ollie is judging the art competition, and his best mate has entered. What is fair? | Tell the teacher and step back from judging his entry | Give his mate top marks, because that's what friends are for | Give his mate low marks to look fair | Judge it secretly and tell no one
exercise: What is it called when a judge's friend is in the contest? | A conflict of interest | A friendly match between pals | A fair vote by the whole class | A team bonus for the friend
exercise: Mum asks, "Are you lot planning something for my birthday?" They are. What is a good answer? | Try a cheeky dodge; it's a question with two sides | Tell her everything about the party | Say "no" and look very serious, so she doesn't guess | Cancel the surprise party completely
exercise: The new kid gets blamed for what Ella's best friend did. "He's new, he'll get over it." What should Ella do? | Push her friend to own up, and tell if she won't | Stay quiet; the new kid doesn't matter as much as her best friend | Blame the new kid too so it's settled | Make friends with the new kid secretly
exercise: Ella says lying is never right; Josh says sometimes. What is the best olympiad answer? | It depends: most agree a lie needs a very strong reason, like saving a life, and the best answer weighs both sides | Ella is simply right; there are no exceptions ever | Josh is simply right; lying is fine whenever it helps | It doesn't matter, because both answers score the same
exercise: What did Augustine and Aquinas teach about lying? | Every lie is wrong | Lies are fine on Sundays | Only big lies are wrong | Lies to friends are fine
test: Your mate says, "Lying to the bullies is the same as lying to your mum." What is a reasoned reply? | No; bullies want to hurt Sam, so they have no right to that information | Yes; every lie is exactly the same | No; lying to your mum is always fine, but lying to bullies never ever is | Yes, so tell the bullies where Sam is
test: "Snitch on me and you're dead," says Zac about the fiver. What is the best-reasoned move? | Urge him to put it back, and tell if he won't, especially if someone else may be blamed | Keep quiet, because of the threat | Take half the fiver to keep quiet | Tell everyone at lunch
test: The man in the park says, "I'm her dad, which way?" Tom isn't sure. What is safest? | Don't point; get an adult or call for help | Point, because he says he's her dad, so he must be | Run away home and try to forget all about it | Follow the man quietly to see what happens next
test: Mia's friend cries, "If you tell, I'll never speak to you again!" about her dad's temper. What wins? | Her safety; tell a trusted adult | The promise; say nothing | Lie for her and hope it stops | Tell her dad she's scared of him
test: A cartoon shows Rahab telling kids, "See, the Bible says lying is always fine!" What is wrong? | The Bible praises her faith and courage; the lie itself is still argued about | Nothing; the Bible says lying is always fine | Rahab never lied in the Bible | The spies lied, not Rahab
test: Brody says, "Everyone's in on it, so it's not a lie if we all say it." What is wrong? | A lie stays a lie even if a crowd says it | Nothing; group lies don't count as real lies | It's only a lie if one person says it on their own | The head always knows the truth anyway, so it's fine
test: Ollie's mate says, "You're my mate, so you'll vote for me, yeah?" What is the best reply? | I'll step back from judging yours, so it's fair for everyone | Course, mates always vote for mates, that's the whole point of mates | No, I'll give you zero to look fair | I'll vote for you, but secretly
test: Mum keeps asking about her birthday, and your sister says, "Lying to Mum is always wrong, so tell her!" What is a reasoned answer? | It depends: a dodge keeps the surprise without a lie, and many think a surprise fib is harmless | She's right, ruin the surprise now | Lie loudly so Mum stops asking | Tell Mum to stop asking questions
test: Ella's friend says, "If you tell, you're not my friend." The new kid is being punished. What is best? | Tell, because the new kid matters as much as her friend | Stay quiet to keep the friendship, because friends come first | Ask the new kid to take the blame | Wait until the new kid leaves
test: The judge asks, "Is it ever right to lie?" and a team just says, "Never, because." Why will they score low? | They gave no reasons and ignored the other side | Because "never" is always the wrong answer | Because judges only like the word "sometimes" in answers | Because the answer was too short to hear
test: Great-Gran asks for her dog again. Your cousin says, "Just tell her he's dead, again." What is a reasoned view? | It's an open question; many carers think gently going along is kinder, others disagree | He's right; truth must be told every time | Tell her the dog ran away | Ignore her questions completely
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the response to a moral dilemma most esteemed by the judges of an ethics olympiad? | A response which furnishes reasons and weighs fairly the considerations on each side | A response which asserts a conclusion without any supporting reasons | A response which accords exclusively with the majority opinion | A response which declines to engage with the question posed
exam: Select the statement consistent with the accounts recorded in Joshua 2:4-6 and Hebrews 11:31. | Rahab concealed the spies and misled the king's men, and her faith is commended | Rahab surrendered the spies to the king's men | Rahab's falsehood is expressly commanded in Scripture as a universal rule for all | Rahab is condemned in Hebrews for her conduct
exam: Given that a child confides fear of violence at home and requests a false alibi, identify the course of action most consistent with this lesson. | The refusal of the falsehood together with the disclosure of the fear to a trusted adult | The provision of the false alibi in the interests of loyalty to the friend who requested it | The confrontation of the parent by the child | The disregard of the matter as a private family affair
exam: Ascertain the term which denotes the position of a judge whose friend is a competitor in the contest being judged. | A conflict of interest | A fair adjudication | A unanimous verdict | A sporting walkover for the friend
